  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nWhile the morning hours begin dry across eastern New York and \nwestern New England, rain shower and thunderstorm activity increases \nthis afternoon from west to east. The Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) \nhas not changed from yesterday's Day 2 Severe Weather Outlook \nby the Storm Prediction Center (SPC) which now became Day 1 that \nincludes the southwestern Adirondacks, Mohawk Valley, and \neastern Catskills. Let's talk about what could happen this \nafternoon into this evening. There are two different scenarios \nthat can happen across the southwestern Adirondacks, western \nMohawk Valley, and eastern Catskills. They are both highly \ndependent on what the stationary front to our west will do with\nthe surface low pressure system. \n\nScenario Number One: The front stalls way to our west and no \nsevere storms develop. This prevents significant warm air \narriving with enough time for surface heating to occur. While \nonly one high resolution model guidance shows this, depending on\nhow this morning's environmental conditions exist this scenario\nshows there could be lacking all necessary ingredients for \nsevere storms to develop (similarly to what happened yesterday) \nacross the southwestern Dacks, Mohawk Valley, and eastern \nCatskills. \n\nScenario Number Two: The front does not stall and we see a few \nsevere storms across southwestern Adirondacks, western Mohawk \nValley, and eastern Catskills. This scenario supports warm air \narriving to these locations for sufficient surface heating. \nAdditionally, there are enough ingredients for a few severe \nstorms to develop in these locations based on the other high \nresolution forecast model guidances. The most likely timing for \nthunderstorms to develop and move into these locations is after \nnoon and through the late afternoon hours. How exactly the \nstorms will be is still the tricky part to today's forecast as a\nfew could develop into a line of storms, while the rest remain \nisolated in nature. \n\nThe current forecast supports scenario number two, but we can't \nignore scenario number 1 for this afternoon into this evening for \nhow the severe weather threat could play out. Forecast confidence \ncontinues to be low for this afternoon into this evening based on \nthe disagreements of the high resolution forecast models and current \nenvironment. While they continue to be in disagreement for how to \nenvironment will play out with the warm front and if we have enough \ningredients needed for storms to become severe, if a severe storm \ndoes develop then the primary concern is going to be damaging wind \ngusts. Continue to monitor latest SPC severe weather outlooks for \ntoday's environment and have multiple ways to receive warnings.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe second concern today with rain showers and thunderstorms is \nheavy rainfall across eastern New York and western New England. \nWhile not everyone will see a severe thunderstorm today, even with \nnon-severe thunderstorms heavy rain is a concern as the environment \nis favorable. Latest high resolution forecast model guidances \nsupport the necessary ingredients needed for heavy downpours \nassociated with rain showers and thunderstorms from late this \nafternoon into the overnight hours tonight. Latest probabilities for \nrainfall totals over 1 inch in the next 24 hours are greater than 75 \npercent for locations north and west of Albany. The ground is \nsaturated from yesterday's rainfall. And if any location sees \nrepeated heavy downpours this evening into tonight, isolated \ninstances of flash flooding could occur. Especially for locations \nthat have typical poor drainage and ponding on roadways. Small \nstream/creeks could rise. \n\nAcross portions of the Mohawk Valley, scattered instances of \nflash flooding have low chances of occurring this evening, but \nthe risk is there as repeated rounds of rain showers and/or \nthunderstorms could occur tonight into Thursday morning. \nRainfall totals for today through tomorrow will greatly depend \non who sees the heaviest rainfall and where repeated rain \nshowers and thunderstorms develop this afternoon into tonight. \nConfidence has increased for the chances of rain showers and \nthunderstorms this afternoon and into the overnight hours, so \nmedium to high chances are in store across eastern New York and \nwestern New England through the early morning hours Thursday.",
